WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.190 --> 00:00:01.370
- [Narrator] Tax season is here

00:00:01.370 --> 00:00:04.330
as the world marks one
year of a global pandemic.

00:00:04.330 --> 00:00:07.490
For many, it was a year of
unprecedented disruption

00:00:07.490 --> 00:00:09.415
in our personal and economic lives.

00:00:09.415 --> 00:00:11.850
- Taxes are, in many ways,

00:00:11.850 --> 00:00:14.340
a reflection of the real
world and the real economy.

00:00:14.340 --> 00:00:16.560
So the real world and the real economy

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:18.223
were a total mess in 2020

00:00:18.223 --> 00:00:19.688
and so the tax season

00:00:19.688 --> 00:00:22.792
that we're having right now reflects that.

00:00:22.792 --> 00:00:23.810
- [Narrator] The US government response

00:00:23.810 --> 00:00:26.180
to the pandemic and
changes in your own life

00:00:26.180 --> 00:00:27.320
could combine to make this

00:00:27.320 --> 00:00:29.770
a potentially tricky year for taxes.

00:00:29.770 --> 00:00:31.720
Here's what you need to know.

00:00:31.720 --> 00:00:35.230
Congress approved two rounds
of stimulus payments in 2020.

00:00:35.230 --> 00:00:38.240
Individuals earning less than $75,000

00:00:38.240 --> 00:00:41.100
and couples earning less than $150,000

00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:42.880
were eligible for the full amount.

00:00:42.880 --> 00:00:44.910
Taken together, adults
at these income levels

00:00:44.910 --> 00:00:47.390
were eligible for $1,800
from the government

00:00:47.390 --> 00:00:51.280
plus an additional $1,100
for every child under 17.

00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:53.230
Many people quickly
received the full amount

00:00:53.230 --> 00:00:54.700
that they were entitled to.

00:00:54.700 --> 00:00:56.040
But others didn't.

00:00:56.040 --> 00:00:58.160
- The IRS has the information that it has

00:00:58.160 --> 00:00:59.810
that you've sent them before, right?

00:00:59.810 --> 00:01:02.100
So when they decide how much to send you

00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:03.410
and they're looking at okay,

00:01:03.410 --> 00:01:06.263
what's your income and
what's your family size,

00:01:06.263 --> 00:01:09.160
they're gonna pull from you
the information they had.

00:01:09.160 --> 00:01:10.382
So in March of 2020,

00:01:10.382 --> 00:01:15.382
they were pulling from 2018
returns and 2019 returns.

00:01:15.800 --> 00:01:18.610
If your income went down in 2020,

00:01:18.610 --> 00:01:21.840
from being ineligible or partly ineligible

00:01:21.840 --> 00:01:25.240
to fully eligible, then you
can use your 2020 tax return

00:01:25.240 --> 00:01:27.250
to claim the rest of that money.

00:01:27.250 --> 00:01:29.230
Let's take an extreme case

00:01:29.230 --> 00:01:34.230
of an individual who made 150,000 in 2019

00:01:34.530 --> 00:01:36.410
and so they didn't get the $1,200 payment,

00:01:36.410 --> 00:01:38.690
they didn't get the $600 payment

00:01:38.690 --> 00:01:40.720
'cause all the IRS knew
is oh, this is someone

00:01:40.720 --> 00:01:42.183
who made 150,000.

00:01:42.183 --> 00:01:45.810
If that person then, in 2020, made 50,000

00:01:45.810 --> 00:01:48.130
and would have qualified
for the full payments,

00:01:48.130 --> 00:01:50.920
then you can go ahead
and claim that full 1,800

00:01:50.920 --> 00:01:52.667
on your 2020 tax return.

00:01:52.667 --> 00:01:54.150
And there's also circumstances

00:01:54.150 --> 00:01:55.820
where your household might have changed.

00:01:55.820 --> 00:01:59.370
So the most common case would
be you had a baby during 2020.

00:01:59.370 --> 00:02:01.087
The IRS would have no
way to know about that

00:02:01.087 --> 00:02:04.230
until you send in your 2020 tax return.

00:02:04.230 --> 00:02:07.382
And so you'll be able
on that to claim $500

00:02:07.382 --> 00:02:08.215
from the first round

00:02:08.215 --> 00:02:10.450
and $600 for the second round.

00:02:10.450 --> 00:02:11.900
- [Narrator] But what if
you're one of the lucky ones

00:02:11.900 --> 00:02:13.510
that had a good 2020?

00:02:13.510 --> 00:02:15.610
Don't worry, you can
get more stimulus money

00:02:15.610 --> 00:02:16.520
if you're owed it

00:02:16.520 --> 00:02:19.460
but the IRS won't take any stimulus back.

00:02:19.460 --> 00:02:21.220
- [Richard] So let's take
an example of someone

00:02:21.220 --> 00:02:24.770
who made 50,000 in 2019.

00:02:24.770 --> 00:02:27.080
They got a full $1,200 check in March,

00:02:27.080 --> 00:02:29.040
a full $600 check in December,

00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:32.800
so that's $1,800 based on
the income that they had

00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:34.650
to qualify within that range.

00:02:34.650 --> 00:02:35.970
And then you had a good 2020.

00:02:35.970 --> 00:02:37.030
You were one of the lucky ones

00:02:37.030 --> 00:02:40.440
and then you made, as
an individual, 120,000.

00:02:40.440 --> 00:02:42.236
You wouldn't have qualified for any money.

00:02:42.236 --> 00:02:43.650
That's okay.

00:02:43.650 --> 00:02:45.580
These payments are not taxable.

00:02:45.580 --> 00:02:47.100
- [Narrator] No matter
what the circumstances are

00:02:47.100 --> 00:02:48.030
that have changed,

00:02:48.030 --> 00:02:49.475
if you're owed more stimulus money,

00:02:49.475 --> 00:02:52.900
you can claim it on line
30 on your tax return.

00:02:52.900 --> 00:02:55.300
There's also now a third
round of stimulus payments

00:02:55.300 --> 00:02:57.070
that Congress passed in March.

00:02:57.070 --> 00:02:59.311
There aren't relevant to your 2020 taxes

00:02:59.311 --> 00:03:02.460
but filing your 2020 taxes
will update the information

00:03:02.460 --> 00:03:04.970
the IRS has so it could
help you get the full amount

00:03:04.970 --> 00:03:06.283
you're entitled to sooner.

00:03:07.280 --> 00:03:09.300
The pandemic closed offices this year

00:03:09.300 --> 00:03:11.220
so many workers set up home offices

00:03:11.220 --> 00:03:13.813
or relocated for more space.

00:03:13.813 --> 00:03:15.260
- [Richard] The IRS, from
the federal perspective,

00:03:15.260 --> 00:03:17.960
if you're working in Maryland
or Virginia or New York

00:03:17.960 --> 00:03:20.100
or New Jersey, it doesn't matter to them

00:03:20.100 --> 00:03:22.090
that federal income is the same.

00:03:22.090 --> 00:03:25.950
But it can have significant
state tax consequences.

00:03:25.950 --> 00:03:27.530
Some states like New York are known

00:03:27.530 --> 00:03:29.640
for being particularly aggressive

00:03:29.640 --> 00:03:32.980
in enforcing their state tax laws

00:03:32.980 --> 00:03:35.420
and trying to expand the boundaries

00:03:35.420 --> 00:03:37.900
of the work that they're taxing.

00:03:37.900 --> 00:03:39.130
Other states may not be.

00:03:39.130 --> 00:03:41.840
In general, if you ended up
spending the year working

00:03:41.840 --> 00:03:43.360
in the state where you live

00:03:43.360 --> 00:03:45.716
as opposed to the one that
you used to commute to,

00:03:45.716 --> 00:03:47.410
it's worth taking some time

00:03:47.410 --> 00:03:50.453
to figure out how that state
pairing works together.

00:03:51.580 --> 00:03:53.210
- [Narrator] Millions of
people lost their jobs

00:03:53.210 --> 00:03:55.290
as a result of the pandemic in 2020

00:03:55.290 --> 00:03:56.630
and Congress funneled assistance

00:03:56.630 --> 00:03:57.920
to many out-of-work people

00:03:57.920 --> 00:03:59.890
through state unemployment systems.

00:03:59.890 --> 00:04:01.120
Many first-time recipients

00:04:01.120 --> 00:04:02.160
were surprised to learn

00:04:02.160 --> 00:04:04.820
that unemployment benefits
are taxable income

00:04:04.820 --> 00:04:07.110
and that they might be
facing a large tax bill

00:04:07.110 --> 00:04:08.510
if they didn't have enough tax money

00:04:08.510 --> 00:04:10.600
withheld from their unemployment benefits.

00:04:10.600 --> 00:04:13.100
But in the law passed in March 2021,

00:04:13.100 --> 00:04:15.660
Congress moved to prevent
these surprise tax bills.

00:04:15.660 --> 00:04:17.910
- [Richard] What Congress did in March,

00:04:17.910 --> 00:04:21.850
retroactively said the first $10,200

00:04:21.850 --> 00:04:24.600
of unemployment benefits per worker

00:04:24.600 --> 00:04:27.260
are exempt from taxes

00:04:27.260 --> 00:04:30.700
for households making up to $150,000,

00:04:30.700 --> 00:04:32.490
which took away that surprise tax burden

00:04:32.490 --> 00:04:33.590
for a lot of people.

00:04:33.590 --> 00:04:36.663
They're gonna save about $25
billion to those households.

00:04:37.550 --> 00:04:39.570
- [Narrator] Since this
law only passed recently,

00:04:39.570 --> 00:04:41.420
some recipients have already paid taxes

00:04:41.420 --> 00:04:45.600
on the first $10,200 of their
unemployment benefits for 2020

00:04:45.600 --> 00:04:47.830
either through withholding
or filing early.

00:04:47.830 --> 00:04:49.980
If you're one of those
people, don't worry.

00:04:51.540 --> 00:04:53.260
- [Richard] If you filed your tax return

00:04:53.260 --> 00:04:56.960
and then basically paid taxes
on your unemployment benefits,

00:04:56.960 --> 00:04:58.960
the IRS says don't do anything,

00:04:58.960 --> 00:05:00.647
don't send in an amended return.

00:05:00.647 --> 00:05:03.440
They're gonna do the math themselves

00:05:03.440 --> 00:05:05.770
and send out refunds to people

00:05:05.770 --> 00:05:07.400
who are in that situation.

00:05:07.400 --> 00:05:09.640
- [Narrator] Thankfully,
the IRS has given taxpayers

00:05:09.640 --> 00:05:11.590
a little more time to
figure out their taxes

00:05:11.590 --> 00:05:14.130
from this strange and difficult year.

00:05:14.130 --> 00:05:15.580
- [Richard] The filing
deadlines this year,

00:05:15.580 --> 00:05:18.810
we're all used to April
15th being April 15th

00:05:18.810 --> 00:05:20.530
but this year, it's not.

00:05:20.530 --> 00:05:23.830
For most people, the deadline
to pay your 2020 taxes

00:05:23.830 --> 00:05:27.280
is May 17th now, not April 15th.

00:05:27.280 --> 00:05:29.830
If you're in Texas, Oklahoma or Louisiana,

00:05:29.830 --> 00:05:31.039
because of the storms,

00:05:31.039 --> 00:05:34.033
all of the deadlines
are pushed to June 15th.

